I dont' know what
	if __name__ == "__main__":
	
means

The if __name__ != "__main__": statement checks if a Python file is being imported as a module into another script, rather than being run directly.

How it works:

• __name__ is a special built-in variable in Python.
• When you run a script directly (e.g., python my_script.py), Python sets __name__ to "__main__".
• When you import the script into another file (e.g., import my_script), Python sets __name__ to the actual name of the module (e.g., "my_script")


• Therefore, if __name__ != "__main__": means: "Run the code inside this block only if this file is being imported by another program, not if it is being run directly."
This is the exact opposite of the very common if __name__ == "__main__": idiom, which runs code only when the file is executed directly.

